\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Symmetry survives the Gaussian marginal}
\date{2026-10-17}

\p{Let a group #{\Gamma} act by a
\vocabk{signed-incidence symmetry}{fdip-003B} on a finite incidence
presentation with positive real weights and nonempty vertex set.
Use the counting pairings, their induced Borel volume and a scalar
mass #{m>0}. The [positive massive precision](fdip-000A) and
[inverse-precision covariance](fdip-000B) are
##{L=d^\dagger Wd,\qquad Q=L+m^2I,\qquad G=Q^{-1}.}
For every #{g\in\Gamma},
##{\begin{aligned}
LP_g&=P_gL,&QP_g&=P_gQ,\\
GP_g&=P_gG,&P_gGP_g^\dagger&=G.
\end{aligned}}
Thus #{Qf=j} implies #{Q(P_gf)=P_gj}. The
[Gaussian law](fdip-0005) is invariant: #{(P_g)_*\mu_Q=\mu_Q}.
For every real or complex Borel observable #{F},
##{\begin{aligned}
\int|F\circ P_g|\,d\mu_Q&=\int|F|\,d\mu_Q,\\
\omega_Q(F\circ P_g)&=\omega_Q(F)
\end{aligned}}
with extended integrals in the first equality, and finite absolute
integral required in the second.}

\proof{
\p{The signed action gives counting isometries and
#{dP_g=J_gd}, #{WJ_g=J_gW}. The
[paired transport theorem](fdip-003D) gives the stated commutations;
#{P_g^\dagger=P_g^{-1}} gives the covariance congruence.
The unique sourced solution is #{f=Gj}. At zero source this is zero,
although the action on the entire field carrier need not be trivial.}

\p{The scalar [Euclidean action](fdip-0004)
#{\mathcal A_Q(f)=b(f,Qf)/2} satisfies
#{\mathcal A_Q(P_gf)=\mathcal A_Q(f)} by pairing isometry and
commutation. This scalar function differs from the group action
#{g\mapsto P_g}. The [Gaussian transport theorem](fdip-000H),
with #{Q'=Q}, proves event-law invariance and the extended absolute
integral before the expectation assertion. Its proof applies to the
weaker incidence intertwiners and therefore to signed endpoint actions;
no directed-endpoint claim is inferred from the weaker hypotheses.}
}

\p{Suppose further that a nonempty proper retained subset
#{R\subsetneq V} is stable under every #{p_g}. Its complement
#{D=V\setminus R} is also stable. The restricted maps
#{P_{g,R},P_{g,D}} give the orthogonal split correspondence.
For the [split components](fdip-001T) #{A,B,E,C}, with
#{E=B^\dagger}, and #{S=A-BC^{-1}E>0}, one has
##{\begin{aligned}
P_{g,R}SP_{g,R}^{-1}&=S,\\
P_{g,R}S^{-1}P_{g,R}^{-1}&=S^{-1},\\
(P_{g,R})_*\mu_S&=\mu_S.
\end{aligned}}
The retained equation #{Sx=k} is equivariant as well. The full fiber
factor #{c_C=(2\pi)^{|D|/2}/\sqrt{\det C}} and the stationary
eliminated field are compatible with the action:
##{y_*(P_{g,R}x)=P_{g,D}y_*(x),\qquad y_*(x)=-C^{-1}Ex.}}

\proof{
\p{The [four-block transport identities](fdip-003E) give
Schur conjugacy, its inverse conjugacy, and stationary-field transport.
Its actual projection identity is #{\pi_RP_g=P_{g,R}\pi_R}.
By the [normalized marginal theorem](fdip-000E) and full-law invariance,
##{\begin{aligned}
(P_{g,R})_*\mu_S
 &=(P_{g,R}\pi_R)_*\mu_Q\\
 &=(\pi_RP_g)_*\mu_Q=\pi_{R*}\mu_Q=\mu_S.
\end{aligned}}
The [real transport theorem](fdip-000H) also proves invariance of
#{\det C}, #{c_C}, the additive action constant and #{Z_S}.
For every integrable retained observable #{f},
##{\begin{aligned}
\omega_S(f\circ P_{g,R})&=\omega_S(f),\\
\omega_Q(f\circ\pi_R)&=\omega_S(f).
\end{aligned}}
The marginal theorem supplies the absolute-integral equivalence
needed here. On actual polynomial functions this is the
[retained functional square](fdip-003F). Gaussian integration proves
the law; algebraic Schur conjugacy by itself does not.}
}

\p{For the half-turn, retain #{R=(0,2)} and eliminate #{D=(1,3)}.
Both restrictions are the nonidentity swap
#{J=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\1&0\end{pmatrix}}. Here #{H} is the array of the split component #{B}. The projection square is}

\tikzfigs{0.75}{\begin{tikzpicture}[
  every node/.style={inner sep=4pt},
  transport/.style={-{Stealth[length=2mm]},shorten >=2pt,shorten <=2pt}
]
\node (v) at (0,0) {$\mathbb R^V$};
\node (vp) at (3.4,0) {$\mathbb R^V$};
\node (r) at (0,-2) {$\mathbb R^{\{0,2\}}$};
\node (rp) at (3.4,-2) {$\mathbb R^{\{0,2\}}$};
\draw[transport] (v) -- node[above=2mm] {$P$} (vp);
\draw[transport] (r) -- node[above=2mm] {$J$} (rp);
\draw[transport] (v) -- node[left=2mm] {$\pi_R$} (r);
\draw[transport] (vp) -- node[right=2mm] {$\pi_R$} (rp);
\end{tikzpicture}}

\p{The lower map swaps the two retained coordinates. Put #{a=m^2}
and #{s=1+t+a}. The [explicit elimination](fdip-000F) gives
##{\begin{aligned}
A=C&=sI_2,\qquad H=-\begin{pmatrix}1&t\\t&1\end{pmatrix},\\
S&=\begin{pmatrix}\alpha&-\beta\\-\beta&\alpha\end{pmatrix},\\
\alpha&=s-\frac{1+t^2}{s},\qquad\beta=\frac{2t}{s}.
\end{aligned}}
All three blocks commute with #{J}, hence #{JSJ=S}. Positivity
follows also from the two eigenvalues
##{\begin{aligned}
\alpha-\beta&=\frac{a(a+2+2t)}s>0,\\
\alpha+\beta&=\frac{(a+2)(a+2t)}s>0.
\end{aligned}}
Thus the same nontrivial action preserves the full and retained laws.
The full covariance obeys #{G_{i+2,j+2}=G_{ij}}. Invariant state
does not mean that each observable is pointwise invariant:
#{\phi\mapsto\phi_0^2} is sent to #{\phi\mapsto\phi_2^2};
they differ at #{\delta_0}, but their expectations agree.}

\p{At #{t=m=1}, direct inversion gives
##{\begin{aligned}
S&=\begin{pmatrix}7/3&-2/3\\-2/3&7/3\end{pmatrix},\\
S^{-1}&=\begin{pmatrix}7/15&2/15\\2/15&7/15\end{pmatrix}.
\end{aligned}}
Both swapped squared coordinates have expectation #{7/15}.
The observable #{\phi_0\phi_2} is itself invariant under the
half-turn and has expectation #{2/15}. These expectations agree
before and after elimination.}

\p{The full and retained half-turns are both faithful: their
nonidentity maps send #{\delta_0} to #{\delta_2}. A general full
faithful action can restrict trivially, for instance when it moves
only eliminated vertices. These two assertions are compatible.}

\p{Three concrete obstructions distinguish the hypotheses:
[keeping fixed target labels after relabeling](fdip-003J),
[breaking the alternating weights](fdip-003K), and
[choosing a nonstable retained subset](fdip-003L).
The last is a failure of descent through that projection and does not
exclude accidental symmetry of a marginal density. Extra mass fields
or reference pairings would require their own preservation hypotheses.}

\p{\citet{Definition 4.1 and p. 58}{milne2025group}
gives the group-action and stable-subset conventions.}

\p{\citet{Proposition B.55, p. 505}{friedli2017statistical}
gives the Gaussian substitution convention.
The incidence, stationary and half-turn computations above use the
proved transport results and the [exact cycle elimination](fdip-000F).}

\p{Mathlib's \leanref{LinearIsometryEquiv.measurePreserving}
supplies isometry-volume preservation. TauCeti's
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.Probability.map_affine_multivariateGaussian}
supports covariance-parameter pushforward for the supplied #{P_g}
or retained projection after the [precision-density bridge](fdip-000B).
These declarations do not assert the endpoint, weight or stable-subset
hypotheses, or the concrete failures just cited.}
